Mobs specify various forms of their own name: "dog", "a dog" and "the dog".

There are actually six translatable versions of each name. We also have the strings "DOG", "A DOG"
and "THE DOG".

The strings in CAPITALS are meant to be used as the OBJECT of a sentence, for example:

    You see my dog
    You see a dog
    You see the dog

The other strings are meant to be used as the SUBJECT of a sentence, for example:

    Your dog is here
    A dog is here
    The dog is here

When you translate the strings, use normal capitalisation rules for your language. For example, in
German all nouns are capitalised, so translate like this:

    "dog"       >   "Hund"
    "a dog"     >   "ein Hund"
    "the dog"   >   "der Hund"

    "DOG"       >   "Hund"
    "A DOG"     >   "ein Hund"
    "THE DOG"   >   "der Hund"

But in Spanish, nouns are not automatically capitalised, so translate like this:

    "dog"       >   "perro"
    "a dog"     >   "un perro"
    "the dog"   >   "el perro"

    "DOG"       >   "perro"
    "A DOG"     >   "un perro"
    "THE DOG"   >   "el perro"
